    "lawCode": "Add §7013, Ins L; add §89-dd, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Insurance Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S4862\n\nSPONSOR: COMRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the insurance law, in relation to establishing a captive\ninsurance program for commuter vans; and to amend the state finance law,\nin relation to establishing the commuter van trust fund\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to direct the executive to deliver\ncommuter van stabilization money allocated in FY23 in a manner conducive\nto enabling a long term commuter van insurance pool that will stabilize\nthe market for several years to come\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAuthorizes superintendent to establish a commuter van captive insurance\nprogram utilizing FY 23 Commuter Van Stabilization Money\n\nOutlines definition of commuter vans\n\nPromulgates insurance limits for commuter vans/insurers enrolled in\nprogram:\n\n-$500,000 combined single limits (CSL) -$50,000 personal injury\nprotection (PIP)\n\n-(Basic) $25,000/$50,000 uninsured motorist coverage(UM/UIM).  Estab-\nlishes money allocated for program will be designated for the NYS commu-\nter van trust fund.\n\nFor claims in excess of $250,000:\n\n-Establishes insurance company(ies) will award 20% of damages for claims\nin first year and an additional 10% each year thereafter.\n\n-Establishes trust fund will award 80% of damages in first year and\ndecrease by 10 percent each year thereafter.\n\nAll no fault insurance related to commuter vans insured in this program\nwill rely on the medical treatment guidelines promulgated in existing\nworkers' compensation law.\n\nAfter 5 years\n\n-Claims activity will be actuarially analyzed\n\n-$ will be reviewed to determine +/-\n\n-Potential to return $ to NYS general fund\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCommuter van numbers in NYC dwindled from 300+ to 50 during COVID 19.\nThis made this insurance market for commuter vans in NYC dicey. In addi-\ntion, many drivers use New Jersey or other out of state plates to oper-\nate in New York City due to NYS insurance costs exceeding\n$30,000-35,000/ year per driver/van. Many commuter vans are being\nbooted/repossessed.\n\nThis bill aims to stabilize the NYS commuter van market, People use\ncommuter vans as rideshare/carpool systems. It brings people to and from\nsuburbs outside New York City, where public transportation systems are\nless viable, and drops them off at popular subway or bus stops to travel\ninto the city.\n\nSince DFS forced the primary insurance provider for commuter vans into\nreceivership years ago, there have been no affordable coverage options\nfor commuter vans.\n\nAs a result, many commuter vans operate off the books and are unregu-\nlated. The Executive Budget provides a new allocation of $11 million to\nstabilize the insurance market and modernizations to vehicles in terms\nof safety and abilities.\n\nThis is a part of a broader plan of DFS for the next 4-5 years, project-\ned to total between $40-50 million. AIPSO, which services the New York\nAutomobile Insurance Plan (the residual market), is not taking a posi-\ntion on this program. Commuter van policyholder enrollment in the NYAIP\nhas declined significantly since insurance coverage rates increased.\nNYAIP covered only 11 vehicles in 2021.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nFY23 Budget Delivery.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
